{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Rat.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Parity","lean:module:Mathlib.NumberTheory.Padics.PadicVal.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":12,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.gap_invariant_under_scalar_transform","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.gap_invariant_under_scalar_transform","kind":"theorem","line":147,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DLadderTwoAdicGrading.lean#L147-L164","doc":"**q-Borel scalar-transport invariance of the grading gap (sorry-free).**\nEvery member of the scalar acceleration family — classical Borel `÷k!`,\nBorel–Leroy `÷(k!)^s` (integer `s`), Jackson q-Borel `÷[k]_Q!` /\n`÷(Q;Q)_k` — acts diagonally on the ℚ(√3) components of\n`d_k = a_k + b_k√3`: both are divided by the *same* scalar `f ∈ ℚ^×`.\nSince `padicValRat p` is a valuation, the signed component gap\n`v_p(a_k) − v_p(b_k)` is exactly invariant under the whole family: the\nℤ/2 bar-parity grading (gap form) survives into every accelerated\nplane — the invariant is *permissive*, it obstructs no resummation\nshape. Compute twin (exact, k ≤ 21, six bases):\n`computations/qbeta/qbeta_dladder_qborel_2adic_transport_probe.py`\n(witness `qbeta-dladder-qborel-2adic-transport`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.graded_window","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.graded_window","kind":"theorem","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DLadderTwoAdicGrading.lean#L139-L145","doc":"Conditional consequence (§3b-cond): *given* the grading class, the finite\nwindow predicate holds at every `K` — the anchor theorem is the `K=5`\ninstance of what the class asserts unconditionally. Derives the conclusion\nfrom the class hypothesis (not a self-assuming projection). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.DLadderGradingConjecture.graded","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.DLadderGradingConjecture.graded","kind":"theorem","line":137,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DLadderTwoAdicGrading.lean#L137-L137","doc":"The 2-adic parity grading, for all `k ≥ 1`. Machine-verified for\n`k ≤ 21` by `bDen_two_adic_graded`; open beyond. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.DLadderGradingConjecture.b","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.DLadderGradingConjecture.b","kind":"def","line":134,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DLadderTwoAdicGrading.lean#L134-L134","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.DLadderGradingConjecture.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.DLadderGradingConjecture.mk","kind":"ctor","line":133,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DLadderTwoAdicGrading.lean#L133-L133","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.DLadderGradingConjecture","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.DLadderGradingConjecture","kind":"class","line":127,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DLadderTwoAdicGrading.lean#L127-L137","doc":"**Open target (§3b conjecture class).** The full bar-odd D-ladder\n`√3`-component `b : ℕ → ℚ` (the `ser_sqrt`/`ser_div` object) is 2-adically\nparity-graded for **all** `k ≥ 1`. Content-bearing (a parameter-quantified\nequivalence in the shape of the claim, not `True`); the class instance is the\nsingle open input. Proving it needs the D-ladder construction in Lean —\naxiomatizing this class is **not** a proof of the law (anti-laundering). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.three_not_parity_graded","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.three_not_parity_graded","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DLadderTwoAdicGrading.lean#L118-L125","doc":"**Contrast: p = 3 is NOT graded (sorry-free).** `3` divides `den(b_k)` at\nboth an **even** index (`k=2`, den `288 = 2⁵·3²`) and an **odd** index\n(`k=3`, den `27 = 3³`), so no parity rule `3 ∣ den(b_k) ⟺ (parity of k)`\ncan hold — unlike `2`, whose grading is `bDen_two_adic_graded`. Together they\nwitness that **`2` is the *unique* graded prime**: only the prime of the ℤ/2\nbar-involution sees the parity grading (p=3 is factorial-smooth growth on both\nparities). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.bDen_two_adic_graded_five","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.bDen_two_adic_graded_five","kind":"theorem","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DLadderTwoAdicGrading.lean#L113-L116","doc":"The `K = 5` window, as a corollary of the extended anchor. Retained because\nthe audit and the surrounding prose cite the five-term result by name. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.bDen_two_adic_graded","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.bDen_two_adic_graded","kind":"theorem","line":98,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DLadderTwoAdicGrading.lean#L98-L111","doc":"**Verified anchor (sorry-free).** The 2-adic parity grading holds for **all\n21 committed exact D-ladder coefficients**: `den(b_k)` is odd iff `k` is odd.\n\nExtended from `K = 5` to `K = 21` on 2026-08-19. The `k = 21` data was already\ncommitted and the previous docstring already said \"compute extends this to\n`k=21`\" — the formalized window simply had not caught up with it. Nothing new\nwas computed; the denominators were re-derived from the committed exact\nfractions and cross-checked against the witness's own valuation field.\n\nThis is evidence, not a proof: `DLadderGradingConjecture` below remains the\nopen target, and 21 orders of `decide` do not establish a law for all `k`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.TwoAdicParityGraded","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.TwoAdicParityGraded","kind":"def","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DLadderTwoAdicGrading.lean#L93-L96","doc":"**2-adic parity grading predicate.** The reduced denominator is odd\n(equivalently `v₂(den) = 0`) exactly on odd indices `1 ≤ k ≤ K`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.bDen","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.bDen","kind":"def","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DLadderTwoAdicGrading.lean#L54-L91","doc":"The **reduced denominators** `den(b_k)` of the exact D-ladder coefficients,\n`k = 1 … 21`, as ℕ data from the compute. Kept as explicit ℕ so the grading is\na pure decidable ℕ fact (ℚ-division does not kernel-reduce under `decide`).\n\n**Provenance.** Each entry is `Fraction(b_k).denominator` for the exact\n`b_k ∈ ℚ` recorded in\n`computations/qbeta/qbeta-dladder-generating-function-structure.witness.json`,\nfield `data.b_k_sqrt3_coeff` (21 orders, `ORD = 43`). The denominators were\nrecomputed from those exact fractions rather than copied from the witness's\nown `v2_den_b` field, so the two are an independent cross-check of each other\nand they agree at every order.\n\nSample, matching `bComp`: `b₁` den 1; `b₂ = 5/288` den `288 = 2⁵·3²`;\n`b₃ = -676/27` den 27; `b₄ = 15055/1741824` den `1741824 = 2¹⁰·3⁴·…`;\n`b₅ = -252241/243` den `243 = 3⁵`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.bComp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DLadderTwoAdicGrading.bComp","kind":"def","line":40,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DLadderTwoAdicGrading.lean#L40-L52","doc":"The exact `√3`-component `b_k` of the bar-odd D-ladder `d_k = a_k + b_k√3`,\ntabulated from the exact ℚ(√3) compute. This is an **illustrative sample** —\nthe first five values, kept in full so a reader can check `bDen` against real\nfractions; `bDen` itself carries all 21 committed orders. The full sequence is\nthe `ser_sqrt`/`ser_div` build, not yet in Lean. Indices `0` and `> 5` pad\nwith `0`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}